# =============================================================================
# Rebuttals_2 .gitignore
#
# Written to avoid the two failures of the Rebuttals/ repo, where these rules
# were added AFTER the offending files had already been committed. A .gitignore
# has no effect on files git is already tracking. If something slips through:
#
#     git rm -r --cached __pycache__ .ipynb_checkpoints && git commit
#
# Check before the first commit, not after:  git status --short
# =============================================================================

# --- Data. Never committed: too large for GitHub, and not ours to redistribute.
# Lives in ../data/ (a SIBLING of this repo) so an accidental `git add -A`
# cannot reach it. These patterns are belt-and-braces.
data/
datasets/
*.csv
*.tsv
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.parquet
*.feather
*.h5
*.hdf5

# --- Generated artefacts.
#
# results/ is TRACKED. Its contents are small .json and .md files, and they are
# how results travel between machines and into the response. An earlier version
# of this file ignored results/ wholesale to keep the tree clean; that was the
# wrong trade, because it silently broke the only channel for getting numbers
# off the training box. Heavy artefacts inside results/ are still excluded by
# the global rules below (*.npz, *.pt, *.csv, ...), which is what the rule was
# actually for.
#
# reported/ is the curated subset: numbers that went into the rebuttal, copied
# there by hand, so the response text stays reproducible from the repo alone.
*.npz
*.npy
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt

# --- Python. These are the ones the old repo tracked by accident.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb_checkpoints
.venv/
venv/
env/
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.mypy_cache/

# --- Editors / OS
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# --- Logs
*.log
nohup.out
wandb/
